response.text:
The key is to prove a conserved sum and establish what the counter value must be when the FSM reaches `DONE`. These three lemmas are individually 1-inductive under the module’s reset and transition logic; together, they imply `prop`.

<json>
{
  "lemmas": [
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_1; @(posedge clk) disable iff (rst) (x + y == n); endproperty",
      "explanation": "Reset sets x=n and y=0. Each loop iteration decrements x and increments y, preserving their fixed-width sum; other transitions leave both unchanged."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_2; @(posedge clk) disable iff (rst) (n >= 0 |-> x >= 0); endproperty",
      "explanation": "For nonnegative n, x starts nonnegative. The loop decrements x only when x>0, so it cannot cross below zero."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_3; @(posedge clk) disable iff (rst) (state == DONE |-> x <= 0); endproperty",
      "explanation": "The FSM enters DONE only when x<=0, and x does not change in DONE. Combined with lemma_2, this gives x=0 for nonnegative n; lemma_1 then gives y=n."
    }
  ]
}
</json>

The proof can be split into a conserved counter sum, a nonnegative countdown, and the condition for entering `DONE`. Together, these invariants imply the target property and can be established by one-step induction from reset.

<json>
{
  "lemmas": [
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_1; @(posedge clk) disable iff (rst) ((x + y) == n); endproperty",
      "explanation": "Reset sets x=n and y=0. Each loop step decreases x and increases y by one; otherwise both hold. This connects the final count to n."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_2; @(posedge clk) disable iff (rst) ((n >= 0) |-> (x >= 0)); endproperty",
      "explanation": "For nonnegative n, x starts nonnegative and is decremented only while positive, so it cannot become negative."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_3; @(posedge clk) disable iff (rst) ((state == DONE) |-> (x <= 0)); endproperty",
      "explanation": "The FSM enters DONE only when x<=0, and x remains unchanged there. With lemma_2, DONE and nonnegative n force x=0; lemma_1 then gives y=n."
    }
  ]
}
</json>

The counter starts with `x = n` and `y = 0`. Each loop step transfers one count from `x` to `y`; it enters `DONE` only when `x <= 0`. The lemmas below capture those facts without restating the target property. They are collectively 1-inductive after reset and imply `y == n` whenever `DONE` and `n >= 0`. The `|->` implications check their consequents in the same cycle. ([accellera.org](https://www.accellera.org/images/resources/videos/SystemVerilog_Assertions_Tutorial_2016.pdf?utm_source=openai))

<json>
{
  "lemmas": [
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_1; @(posedge clk) disable iff (rst) (x + y == n); endproperty",
      "explanation": "Reset establishes the sum, and each loop step decrements x while incrementing y. No other non-reset transition changes the sum."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_2; @(posedge clk) disable iff (rst) (n >= 0) |-> (x >= 0); endproperty",
      "explanation": "For nonnegative n, x starts nonnegative and is decremented only when positive, so it cannot become negative."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_3; @(posedge clk) disable iff (rst) (state == DONE) |-> (x <= 0); endproperty",
      "explanation": "The FSM enters DONE only when x is nonpositive, then leaves x unchanged. Together with lemma_2, this establishes x == 0 in DONE for nonnegative n."
    }
  ]
}
</json>
